## 🔹 What is an Attribute?

An **attribute** is a variable or data associated with an object or class.

Example:

```python
class Student:
    college = "KU"

    def __init__(self, name, age):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age

Here:

name and age are instance attributes
college is a class attribute
🔹 Accessing Attributes

Attributes can be accessed using the dot (.) operator.

s1 = Student("Namratha", 20)

print(s1.name)
print(s1.age)
print(s1.college)

Output:

Namratha
20
KU
🔹 Modifying an Instance Attribute

An instance attribute can be changed for a particular object.

s1.name = "Anu"

print(s1.name)

Output:

Anu

This change affects only s1.

🔹 Modifying a Class Attribute

A class attribute can be modified using the class name.

Student.college = "ABC College"

print(Student.college)

Output:

ABC College

The change is reflected when objects access the class attribute, unless they have their own attribute with the same name.

🔹 __dict__ in Python

__dict__ shows the attributes stored in an object's or class's namespace.

Example:

class Student:
    college = "KU"

    def __init__(self, name, age):
        self.name = name
        self.age = age

s1 = Student("Namratha", 20)

print(s1.__dict__)

Output:

{'name': 'Namratha', 'age': 20}

name and age are stored in s1 because they are instance attributes.

The class attribute college belongs to the Student class.

