🔹 Attribute Lookup

When we access an attribute:

s1.name

Python looks for the attribute.

Basic lookup:

Python checks the object (s1).
If it is not found, Python checks the class (Student).
If it is still not found, Python checks through inheritance.
If the attribute cannot be found, Python raises AttributeError.

Example:

class Student:
    college = "KU"

s1 = Student()

print(s1.college)

college is not stored in s1, so Python finds it in the Student class.

🔹 Built-in Functions for Working with Attributes Dynamically

Python provides built-in functions to get, set, check, and delete attributes dynamically.

1. getattr()

Used to get an attribute.

print(getattr(s1, "college"))
2. setattr()

Used to set or modify an attribute.

setattr(s1, "name", "Namratha")

This is similar to:

s1.name = "Namratha"
3. hasattr()

Used to check whether an attribute exists.

print(hasattr(s1, "name"))

Output:

True
4. delattr()

Used to delete an attribute.

delattr(s1, "name")

This is similar to:

del s1.name
🔹 Example Using All Four Functions
class Student:
    pass

s1 = Student()

setattr(s1, "name", "Namratha")

print(hasattr(s1, "name"))
print(getattr(s1, "name"))

delattr(s1, "name")

print(hasattr(s1, "name"))

Output:

True
Namratha
False
